# ADR: Establish the result-first core contract

## Status
Accepted

## Context
The prototype mixed natural-language parsing, model confidence, terminal presentation, and execution authority. Before public release, the project needed an explicit one-action contract with exact output and deterministic local policy.

## Decision
Adopt the Plan 1 contract: opaque intent parsing, typed proposals and effects, default execution with advisory pauses, byte-exact command handling, strict configuration and paths, static trusted instructions, and replaceable runtime seams. Rust 1.82 is the MSRV.

## Alternatives Considered
- Preserve the prototype flags and compatibility: rejected because there are no public users and the old grammar could change authority from prompt text.
- Keep hand-written JSON/YAML parsers: rejected because correctness at the execution boundary matters more than dependency count.
- Treat the detector as a safety gate: rejected because incomplete shell classification cannot justify a safety promise.

## Consequences
The initial public history starts from a deliberately breaking but tested foundation. The Responses API, bounded repair loop, context modes, metadata receipts, telemetry, and release packaging remain staged in later plans.
